-- A refined CONSTRUCTOR-TAG contract COMPOSES across a call boundary, exactly
-- as the `len` measure contract of
-- `accept/t128_refine_list_contract_composes_call` does: a function that
-- requires `{Option(Int) | is_Some(_)}` may pass that very parameter on to
-- another function with the identical requirement, and the callee's
-- obligation is PROVED from the caller's own declared contract alone -- no
-- `match` narrowing and no runtime guard at either call site.
--
-- Before 2026-07-29 the measure shape composed and this one did not, which is
-- the sort of gap only a counted witness finds. `Option` WAS admitted to the
-- refined scope channel (`refined_scope_ty` accepts every registered ADT), so
-- `outer`'s promise about `p` did produce a scope entry -- but nothing
-- consulted it for a TAG: `reflect_dt`'s `EVar` arm declared `p` as a fresh,
-- unconstrained datatype constant, so `((_ is Some) p)` was satisfiable both
-- ways and `inner(p)` inside `outer`'s body was SKIPPED. `outer(Some(1))`
-- from `main` still proved on its own, so the file exited 0 either way and
-- the missing half was invisible.
--
-- That is why this file is bracketed by the rejecting
-- `reject/t130_refine_tag_composition_narrowed_violation`: it shows the SAME
-- two-hop shape does not blind the checker to a real failure once the
-- caller's body narrows the value against its own contract. Exit 0 here
-- cannot be explained away as "the checker accepts whatever it cannot
-- disprove".
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md and the `compose-tag` suite in
-- `test/test_refinecheck.ml`, which asserts the obligation COUNTS (2 proved,
-- 0 skipped) that this file's exit code cannot show.
mod T129 do

  fn inner(o : {Option(Int) | is_Some(_)}) : Int do
    match o do
      Some(v) -> v
      None    -> 0
    end
  end

  fn outer(p : {Option(Int) | is_Some(_)}) : Int do
    inner(p)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    outer(Some(1))
  end

end
